            <ArticleTitle>Trigeminal autonomic cephalgias and variants: clinical profile in Indian patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>859-66</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The present study summarizes the authors' experience of the clinical profile of short-lasting trigeminal autonomic cephalgias (TAC) in Indian patients. Over a period of 17 years a total of 41 cases of episodic cluster headache, seven cases of chronic cluster headache, six cases of variant cluster headache, three cases of paroxysomal hemicrania, and a single case of SUNCT syndrome were encountered. TACs appear to be rare in Indian patients and cluster headache seems to be exclusively a disease of men. The present report is to the best of our knowledge the first of its kind to be reported from India.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
